WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.440
When it comes to horror movies, not every
shrill shriek from its stars is fake.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.050
More often than not, production on a thriller
pic is as smooth-sailing as any other genre

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:15.130
film, but every so often the fright factor
bleeds over into real life.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:20.060
In the case of these scary movies, some of
their cast members were left shaken by what

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.420
they experienced on set.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.069
A Clockwork Orange

00:00:24.069 --> 00:00:29.429
Stanley Kubrick's highly controversial adaptation
of A Clockwork Orange is disturbing enough

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:32.500
for the viewer, let alone for the actor who
starred in it.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.160
"I've taught you much, my little droogies."

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.100
For the famous brainwashing scene in the film,
lead actor Malcolm McDowell allowed his eyes

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.480
to be pinned open for every take.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.710
Unfortunately, McDowell didn't leave unscathed:
the physical torment caused him to suffer

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:53.140
from temporary blindness and a scratched cornea,
and it took decades for him to be able to

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.679
watch the film and actually enjoy Kubrick's
work.

00:00:56.679 --> 00:01:00.379
"I would like to smash 'em."

00:01:00.379 --> 00:01:01.839
The Birds

00:01:01.839 --> 00:01:06.980
While filming The Birds, Alfred Hitchcock
absolutely tormented his star actress, Tippi

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.340
Hedren, both on- and off-set.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:15.170
Through manipulation, sexual slurs, and obsessive
behaviors, the notoriously tough director

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.869
made Hedren live to regret her decision to
star in his film.

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:22.690
The tipping point for Hedren, though, was
when Hitchcock lied about the use of real

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.020
birds in a particular scene.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.759
Instead of the mechanical birds used in the
rest of the film, Hedren was duped into acting

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:33.689
with live ones that were hurled at her over
a week of filming.

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:37.310
She left the set bruised, bloodied, and emotionally
scarred.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.470
"They really didn't need five days of that.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:44.220
I don't know what it was, cruelty?

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.189
It was mean."

00:01:46.189 --> 00:01:47.189
Psycho

00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:52.180
Tippi Hedren wasn't the only one to experience
a real-life fright on the set of a Hitchcock

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.180
film.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:57.670
Janet Leigh was never quite the same after
starring as Marion Crane in Psycho either.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:02.240
She was so traumatized after watching herself
in the famous shower scene that she became

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.429
too afraid to take showers and from that point
forward, and only took baths.

00:02:06.429 --> 00:02:11.110
"I don't take showers unless there's no other
way to bathe, and in that case the door is

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.230
open, if they're sliding doors, the curtain
is open.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.440
If it's a curtain and the door to the bathroom
is open, so that I can see out."

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.000
Rosemary's Baby

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.410
Mia Farrow went through quite a few traumatizing
experiences while working on Roman Polanski's

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.890
American film debut, Rosemary's Baby.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:32.410
A strict vegetarian, Farrow was asked by Polanski
if she wouldn't mind eating raw chicken liver

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:33.660
for a scene.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:38.910
Little did she know she would be forced to
consume it over and over again for different

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:39.910
takes.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.360
That's the kind of shooting experience that
definitely leaves a bad taste in an actress'

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.800
mouth.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.630
The Blair Witch Project

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:50.690
While The Blair Witch Project is a movie that's
probably laughed at more than it actually

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:56.460
scares people nowadays, the disturbing ways
in which the directors haunted the cast are

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.360
frightening enough on their own.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.840
Filmed over an eight-day camping trip, there
was little interaction between the cast and

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:03.840
directors.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.290
Sure, they had daily meetings to provide the
actors with more supplies and instructions

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:12.540
for where to go next, but the trio that starred
in the pic were mostly on their own.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.520
"You've got to be kidding me, this is a joke!"

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.440
To authentically scare the cast, directors
also stalked the actors during the day, rattled

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:25.430
their tents while they were sleeping at night,
and intentionally gave them less food as the

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:30.670
days progressed so they became more and more
angry and exhausted.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.870
The Exorcism of Emily Rose

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:37.330
Jennifer Carpenter might've been fine with
all the sinister surgical procedures going

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:41.890
down on Dexter, but the actress got a case
of the spooks during the production of The

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:43.540
Exorcism of Emily Rose.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:48.100
While she was filming the religious thriller,
some strange things started happening to her

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.100
after hours.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.280
She told Dread Central,

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.430
"Two or three times when I was going to sleep
my radio came on by itself.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.820
The only time it scared me was once because
it was really loud, and it was Pearl Jam's

00:03:58.820 --> 00:03:59.870
'Alive.'"

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.190
She wasn't the only one to experience such
supernatural phenomena on the set, either;

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:09.160
co-star Laura Linney's television repeatedly
turned itself on at night during the shoot.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.190
Carpenter later chalked up these happenings
to some electrical issue with the hotel where

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:14.190
they were staying …

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:19.040
"My stereo would come on really loudly and
I wasn't listening to it, I didn't know if

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.289
there was a short in the plug or what, but
it happened."

00:04:23.289 --> 00:04:28.699
But she was also really glad the clock didn't
read 3:00 AM when it happened, since that

00:04:28.699 --> 00:04:32.960
was such a horrifyingly significant time in
the movie.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:34.240
The Conjuring

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.490
The real-life ghost-hunting history of Ed
and Lorraine Warren is freaky enough to put

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:39.760
anyone on edge.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:44.919
But Vera Farmiga knew very little about her
real-life character counterpart before signing

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:50.169
the dotted line on The Conjuring and receiving
her first paranormal encounter as a bonus.

00:04:50.169 --> 00:04:54.430
The actress revealed that during her first
round of research into Warren's work she had

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:58.669
a brush with the beyond after she hung up
the phone with director James Wan.

00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:03.430
As she told Cinema Blend, she opened her laptop
to find "three digital claw marks, from the

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:05.860
upper right diagonal to the lower left."

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.539
Those marks would make a second unsettling
arrival in Farmiga's Conjuring experience

00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:14.960
the day after she finished shooting the first
film and returned to her home in New York.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:18.169
She woke up to find a similar set of markings
on her thigh.

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:19.169
She explained,

00:05:19.169 --> 00:05:23.520
"It was these three, very distinct, what looks
like claw marks, that long nails [or] thin

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.319
fingertips could make."

00:05:25.319 --> 00:05:29.819
She chose not to "give into the fear" of the
moment and signed on for the second film,

00:05:29.819 --> 00:05:33.060
which would give a different castmate a case
of the creeps.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.439
Patrick Wilson was no newbie to the scream
scene before he joined The Conjuring series,

00:05:37.439 --> 00:05:42.060
but the second installment of his ghost-hunting
endeavor did freak him out the most.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.689
According to him, there was a "pretty trippy"
moment during production involving a curtain

00:05:46.689 --> 00:05:50.699
haunting that led the crew to bring in a priest
to bless the set.

00:05:50.699 --> 00:05:51.699
He told Metro,

00:05:51.699 --> 00:05:56.310
"It was a huge curtain that went from the
floor to the ceiling, which was sort of waving

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:59.469
violently and there was no door open or fan
on, no nothing."

00:05:59.469 --> 00:06:05.090
"You didn't even hear any air, but you watched
these curtains sort of violently going."

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:09.849
Wilson considers himself a skeptic, but all
of his supernatural screenplay may have gotten

00:06:09.849 --> 00:06:13.840
the better of him at this point, because he's
admitted to believing his own house may be

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:14.840
haunted now.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:15.840
He told The Independent,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.289
"I've heard people on two different occasions
say they've heard kids' laughter in the middle

00:06:20.289 --> 00:06:22.040
of the night in my house."

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:23.729
Talk about taking work home with you.

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:24.729
Thanks for watching!

